Subject: a scroll found in a redraw moves the whole content, not the part between look-alike rows, and leaves a scrollbar and a sidebar in place

0010 finds the scroll a program draws by repainting its rows (pi's full-screen view, OpenCode)
and animates the span of rows it explains, bounded by rows drawn the same in both frames: a
header, a prompt. OpenCode paints every cell of its pane, so its empty lines are drawn rows that
look alike, and so are a table's rules; scrolled by three rows (a wheel step), an empty line
often lands on an empty line and a rule on the same table's next rule. Each such row looked like
a header that stayed and split the transcript, and only the largest piece slid while the rest
jumped: rows drew over each other and gaps opened where the pieces met, on almost every step (a
screen recording from the user, 2026-09-30; replaying OpenCode's own bytes showed the transcript
cut into two to eight pieces in most frames).

A row now counts as having stayed only when it is drawn as before and the shift would have put
something else there, and something more than a cell or two different (`Shifted.stayed`,
`RowCells`); a row that looks like the one the shift brings moved with the rest. A row the shift
brought in from beyond the screen has nothing to be judged against and never stayed. The span is
widened by the `lines` rows the scroll brought in, even past a row that seems to have stayed,
since an incoming empty line can land on an empty line whose source is the prompt.

OpenCode also draws a scrollbar three cells in from the right, and on a wide window its sidebar
beside the transcript. Neither moves with the text, so the rows beside the thumb or the
sidebar's lines matched nothing: they split the span or ended it early (the last row then showed
its new line at once while the rows above still slid towards it), and inside the region the
thumb and the sidebar's lines slid away with the text and snapped back on every step. The
renderer now keeps each cell's hash of the previous frame (`cellHashes`, `CellGrid`) rather than
each row's. Rows are compared on their middle first, `still_edge_band` (8) columns in from each
side, and on their whole width only when that finds nothing (lines that differ only at the
sides). Then the columns at either side that stayed while the rows moved are left out of the
region (`movingColumns`), from the edge inwards up to the first column seen moving and never more
than half the width: a column is judged by its edges, the cells that differ from the one above,
across the region and the changed rows next to it (`reach_top`, `reach_bottom`); an edge the
shift explains moved, one where it was or a row or two from it (a thumb moving by itself) stayed
(in a row brought in, only one exactly where it was). A column showing neither stays out when
the last region left it out, or when it is a bar within 8 columns of the side or of a still
column (`isBar`: one background, no text, down all the rows but two, that its neighbours don't
have: a scrollbar's track; the thumb changes its glyph as it moves half a cell, so on a scroll's
first step its edges often match nothing). `cellHashes` marks text cells in the low bit for it.
The rows are then compared again without those columns.

0010 kept a region found within two rows of the last one as the last one, so that its motion
stays one animation; with a frame often finding a row too few (one beside the thumb matched
nothing), the first frame's shortfall stayed for the whole scroll, and that row showed its new
line at once on every step while the rows above it slid. The region now takes the rows of both
(`joinInferred`), and the animation under way moves to it, new columns included, rather than a
second one starting over the same rows.

When frames come late (58 ms apart at the start of a fast flick), the content can move further
than the inference looks (half the screen) and no scroll is found; the animation under way then
went on with its offset and ghost rows over content that had jumped, an old row creeping below
the new text. A redraw that changes most of the last inferred region with no scroll found now
ends the animations over it (`mostlyChanged`), and the new content is drawn where it is, as with
motion off.

The inference runs in updateFrame under the renderer state mutex, while drawing ticks and removes
the animations under the draw mutex, on the main thread too; it changes no animation itself. What
should happen to one goes with the scroll (`PendingScroll.from`, the region of the animation to
move) or in `pending_region_end`, and `applyRegionScrolls` does it under the draw mutex.

Calm's own patch (not from thdxg/ghostty); changes 0010's inference, and applies on top of 0014.

