+/// The columns of `rows` that moved with them: all of them, but for a band
+/// at either side that stayed where it was while they moved, such as a
+/// scrollbar beside the text (OpenCode's, three columns in from the right)
+/// or a panel (OpenCode's sidebar). Left in the region, a scrollbar's thumb
+/// slid away with the text and snapped back on every step, and so did the
+/// sidebar's lines. A column is judged by its edges, the cells that differ
+/// from the one above, across the rows' reach (the rows beside a sidebar's
+/// lines match nothing, so the region may start below them): an edge the
+/// shift
+/// explains moved; one where it was, or a row or two from it (a thumb that
+/// moves by itself), stayed (in a row brought in, only one exactly where it
+/// was); a column with more that stayed is still. From
+/// each side inwards, the band ends at the first column seen moving; within
+/// it, a column with no edges either way (a plain track, a margin) is still
+/// when the last region left it out (`keep`), or when it is a bar within
+/// `still_edge_band` columns of the side or of a still column (`isBar`: a
+/// scrollbar's track, beside the text, maybe inside a sidebar).
+/// Never less than half the width.
+fn movingColumns(old: CellGrid, new: CellGrid, rows: MovedRows, keep: ColumnSpan) ColumnSpan {
+    const cols = new.cols;
+    const full: ColumnSpan = .{ .left = 0, .right = cols - 1 };
+    const right = stillEdge(old, new, rows, .right, if (keep.right + 1 < cols) keep.right + 1 else null);
+    const left = stillEdge(old, new, rows, .left, if (keep.left > 0) keep.left - 1 else null);
+    const moving: ColumnSpan = .{
+        .left = if (left) |x| x + 1 else 0,
+        .right = if (right) |x| x -| 1 else cols - 1,
+    };
+    if (moving.right < moving.left or (moving.right - moving.left + 1) * 2 < cols) return full;
+    return moving;
+}
